; http.library's words for a request that failed, as Fault writes them:
; a clause without its full stop, to stand after "Program: " or inside
; a program's own sentence, so it starts in lower case. http.library is
; built with these, and a translation in
; LOCALE:Catalogs/<language>/sys/http.catalog stands in for them. The
; values are put in by RawDoFmt, which takes them in order: a
; translation keeps them so.
#version 1
;
; What each HTTPERR_* means.
MSG_NOMEM (1//)
no memory
MSG_IO (//)
the connection failed
MSG_IO_BECAUSE (//)
the connection failed: %s
MSG_STOPPED (//)
stopped
MSG_TIMEOUT (//)
the server did not answer in time
MSG_URL (//)
not an http:// or https:// URL, or one too long
MSG_HOST (//)
the host has no address
MSG_HOST_NAMED (//)
%s has no address
MSG_TLS (//)
the TLS session failed
MSG_TLS_CERTIFICATE (//)
the server's certificate is not trusted: %s
MSG_TLS_CLOCK (//)
the clock is not set, so no certificate can be checked (TimeSync)
MSG_TLS_ALERT (//)
the server refused the TLS session (alert %u)
MSG_TLS_HANDSHAKE (//)
the TLS handshake failed
MSG_PROTOCOL (//)
the answer is not HTTP
MSG_REDIRECT (//)
more redirects than were allowed, or one to nowhere
MSG_TRUNCATED (//)
the connection ended before the answer did
MSG_STATE (//)
a call out of its turn, or a request that cannot be made
MSG_LIBRARY (//)
a library the request needs is not there
MSG_LIBRARY_NAMED (//)
%s is not there
MSG_DATA (//)
the server's packed answer does not unpack
MSG_CLOSED (//)
the server closed the connection without answering
MSG_UNKNOWN (//)
the request failed (%d)
;
; Why a server's certificate is not trusted: what MSG_TLS_CERTIFICATE
; puts after its colon.
MSG_VERDICT_MALFORMED (//)
one of its certificates cannot be read
MSG_VERDICT_UNKNOWN_ISSUER (//)
no trusted root signed it
MSG_VERDICT_BAD_SIGNATURE (//)
a signature in its chain is wrong
MSG_VERDICT_UNSUPPORTED (//)
it is signed in a way that cannot be checked
MSG_VERDICT_EXPIRED (//)
it has expired
MSG_VERDICT_NOT_YET_VALID (//)
it is not valid yet
MSG_VERDICT_NOT_CA (//)
it was issued by a certificate that may not issue
MSG_VERDICT_PATH_LENGTH (//)
its chain is longer than its CAs allow
MSG_VERDICT_NAME_CONSTRAINTS (//)
a CA in its chain has name constraints
MSG_VERDICT_WRONG_USAGE (//)
it is not for a server
MSG_VERDICT_WRONG_NAME (//)
it is for another host
MSG_VERDICT_TOO_LONG (//)
its chain is too long
MSG_VERDICT_OTHER (//)
for no reason given
